  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Many languages form quality nouns with one regular ending; English chooses between **-ness**, **-ity**, **-th**, and **-dom**, so it is safest to learn each noun as a word, though **-ness** itself is added freely to many adjectives.",
    "grammar": "Nouns in **-ness** are normally uncountable: no plural and no **a** (*kindness*, not *a kindness*), except for a single act or feature (*a kindness*, *a weakness*, *two weaknesses*). A final **y** becomes **i**: *happy*, *happiness*.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Always unstressed, *nəs*; the stress of the adjective does not move: *HAPpy*, *HAPpiness*."
  },
